==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈfɹeɪɡə(ɹ)/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-fragor.wav|Audio (RP)}}

===Etymology 1===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|la|fragor||a breaking to pieces}}, from {{m|la|frangō||to break}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A [[loud]] and [[sudden]] [[sound]]; the [[report]] of anything [[burst]]ing; a [[crash]].
#* '''1709''', {{w|Isaac Watts}}, ''Horae Lyricae''
#*: The direful '''fragor''', when some southern blast / Tears from the Alps a ridge of knotty oaks{{...}}

===Etymology 2===
From the above, by confusion with the root of {{m|en|fragrant}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete|proscribed}} A strong or sweet [[scent]]; [[fragrance]].
#: {{rfquotek|en|Sir T. Herbert}}

{{Webster 1913}}

